From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:35:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Raise the ready state again when a seek leaves the
 end of the media

When playback reaches the end of the media resource, the media element
lowers its ready state to HAVE_CURRENT_DATA. Nothing raised it again:
the only other place that raises the ready state, a state change of the
player, only did so from HAVE_METADATA. So once a media element had
played to its end, it stayed a blocked media element, was never
potentially playing again and never asked the player to play. Calling
play() after `ended` seeked back to the start and fired `seeking`,
`waiting` and `seeked`, but playback never restarted and the play()
promise never settled; seeking back first and calling play() later
hung the same way. This happened with the GStreamer and the OpenHarmony
AVPlayer backends alike, which both accept a seek and a play request
after the end of the stream. `loop` was not affected, because it seeks
back before the ready state is lowered.

Once the media engine completed a seek back from the end, raise the
ready state to HAVE_FUTURE_DATA, unless the element is in an error
state. This reflects that data at the new position is available after
the seek completed, not a measured buffer level. It fires `canplay`,
and `playing` if the element is not paused, resolves the play()
promise and makes the element ask the player to play. Raise it on to
HAVE_ENOUGH_DATA, which fires `canplaythrough`, when the player reports
that it plays. Going to HAVE_ENOUGH_DATA right after the seek would run
the autoplay steps: the can autoplay flag of an element with an
`autoplay` attribute stays set when it ends by itself, so seeking it
back after the end, or setting `currentTime = 0` in its `ended`
handler, would play it again, the latter forever.

A seek back after the end while paused now fires `canplay` too.
Buffering is still not mapped to the ready state, so a stall after the
seek doesn't fire `waiting`.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: a new test, playing-the-media-resource/play-after-ended.html.
Its video subtest passes, and its audio subtest fails on Linux, where
the GStreamer backend reports no seekable range for Ogg resources, so
play() can't seek back; that is unchanged. Not running the autoplay
steps again is Servo's choice, since the spec allows it, so a
Servo-specific test, mozilla/media-autoplay-seek-after-ended.html,
covers it.

Validation: host release builds (`./mach build --release`) of ohos-main and of ohos-main with the previous patch, this one and the two that follow it, a page whose muted `<video>` plays a 30 s, 14 MB H.264 MP4 from a local server and calls play() in its `ended` handler: without the patches, `seeked` fired with `readyState` 2 and playback did not restart within 110 s; with them, the video replayed and fired `ended` again, twice in a row with range support and once without, seeking back after the end and calling play() 1 s after `seeked` works too, and `loop` still wraps. A muted `<video autoplay>` whose `ended` handler sets `currentTime = 0` played once and stayed paused with `readyState` 3 after `canplay` and `seeked`; with an earlier version of this patch that raised the ready state to HAVE_ENOUGH_DATA, it played five times in 5 s, and media-autoplay-seek-after-ended.html fails. A HiHope DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0, API 26) with headless release builds (`./mach build --ohos --release --headless-binary`): the same replays and seeks back, and the `autoplay` video stays paused. `./mach test-wpt --release --timeout-multiplier 2` of media-elements and the-video-element (434 tests) with the patches and without: the new tests pass with them, except for the audio subtest above, and time out without them; loop-from-ended.tentative.html, track-cues-seeking.html, track-cues-sorted-before-dispatch.html, track-remove-by-setting-innerHTML.html, src_object_blob.html and video_timeupdate_on_seek.html differed between the two runs: re-run three times in isolation with and without the patches, src_object_blob.html passed each time, and the others failed or timed out in some runs of either build with the same messages (each subtest of video_timeupdate_on_seek.html, expected to time out, passes in 3 or 4 of 10 runs either way); media_fragment_seek.html fails in most runs on this host either way. `./mach test-wpt --ohos --processes 1` on the DAYU200 of the 60 media tests of the series, the two new tests, loop-from-ended.tentative.html, video_loop_base.html and audio_loop_base.html: the 60 tests give the same results as an earlier run of ohos-main, and every subtest of the others passes, the audio subtest included. A QuickJS host build (`--js-engine quickjs`) of the same tree gives the same results as SpiderMonkey for the two new tests, three runs out of three.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; root-caused, fixed, reworked after review and tested by the agent on Linux and on the device)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
